FSCK(8)                        P32W Unix                         FSCK(8)

NAME
       fsck - file system consistency check

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/fsck [-q] [-y] [-c] [special]

DESCRIPTION
       fsck checks the filesystem on the block device special, by
       default /dev/disk (the root filesystem on the CompactFlash
       card); /dev/ram is the other choice. Without -y it only
       reports. /etc/rc runs fsck -q at every boot.

       fsck first calls sync(2), then reads the device through the
       kernel's buffer cache, so it sees what the kernel sees and
       may be run on a mounted filesystem, the root included.

       It checks, in four passes:

       1.  Every inode in use has a known type (file, directory,
           character or block device, pipe); every block an inode
           owns, directly or through its single-, double- and
           triple-indirect blocks, lies in the data area and is
           owned only once.

       2.  Every directory's size is a multiple of 32; it holds .
           naming itself and an entry ..; and each entry names an
           inode in range that is in use.

       3.  Each inode's link count equals the number of directory
           entries naming it.

       4.  The free-block bitmap marks exactly the blocks before
           the data area and the blocks some inode owns; the
           superblock's counts of free blocks and free inodes are
           right.

       Each problem is printed on a line of its own, for example

              block 312 owned twice (again by inode 40)
              inode 17: link count 2, 1 entries
              block 900: the bitmap says used

       and at the end a summary:

              fsck: 212 inodes, 2933 data blocks, clean

OPTIONS
       -q     quiet: print the summary only if something is wrong.

       -c     check only if the filesystem was not shut down cleanly
              (halt and reboot mark it clean): for a mounted one, as it
              was when mounted (ustat(2)). /etc/rc runs fsck -q -c, so
              a full check happens at boot only after a crash.

       -y     repair what is found (answering yes to every repair):

              -  an inode with a bad type, a block out of range, or
                 a block another inode already owns, is cleared
                 (inode N cleared), and the directory entries
                 naming it are removed;

              -  a directory entry naming a free inode is removed
                 (entry removed);

              -  an inode no directory entry names is cleared
                 (unreferenced: cleared); other wrong link counts
                 are set to the number of entries (set to N);

              -  the free-block bitmap and the superblock's free
                 counts are rebuilt from what the inodes own.

              The summary then says REPAIRED. A mounted filesystem
              other than / is refused (umount it first). The root
              filesystem cannot be unmounted, and the kernel holds
              some of its blocks in its cache; so after repairing
              /, fsck says

                 fsck: / was repaired: restarting without writing
                 the cache back

              and restarts the machine at once with
              reboot(RB_REBOOT|RB_NOSYNC), leaving the repaired
              disk as fsck wrote it (as V7's fsck did). At the
              monitor, b boots again.

EXAMPLES
       /etc/fsck                     check the root filesystem
       /etc/fsck /dev/ram            check the RAM disk
       /etc/fsck -y                  check and repair /, restarting
                                     if anything was repaired

EXIT STATUS
       0 if the filesystem is clean, or -y repaired it; 1 if a
       problem was found (without -y); 2 for a usage error, a
       mounted filesystem with -y, a device that cannot be opened,
       read or written or that holds no filesystem, or not enough
       memory for the check.

FILES
       /dev/disk      the root filesystem
       /dev/ram       the RAM disk

SEE ALSO
       fs(5), reboot(2), mkfs(8), mount(8), df(1), sync(1), rc(8)

BUGS
       There is no -n or -p, and no asking before each repair: -y
       repairs everything. A directory with a bad size, or without
       . or .., is reported but not repaired.

       The superblock's clean flag is not set (-c only reads it);
       the kernel warns about an unclean one at boot. A file's size
       is not checked against the blocks it holds, and there is no
       lost+found.

       Only root can run it (it is mode 700 and the devices are
       readable only by root).
